# Shared memories without undefined behaviour: the soundness half of SET on Pulley
#
# Applies on top of wasm/wasmtime-set-threads.patch and wasm/pulley-atomics.patch, against the
# wasmtime-set tree. It does NOT lift the THREADS refusal - see wasm/PULLEY-ATOMICS.md for what is
# still outstanding before that may move.
#
# What it changes, and why each one is a soundness fix rather than a tidy-up:
#
#   pulley/src/interp.rs
#     Ordinary loads and stores were width-sized relaxed atomics when the address was aligned and
#     byte-wise only when it was not. That is still UB by a second route: Rust forbids MIXED-SIZE
#     atomic accesses to overlapping locations, and `i32.load` beside `i32.load8_u` at the same
#     address is exactly that. Now every ordinary access is byte-wise, which is the only width at
#     which two accesses to one address can never disagree about their size.
#
#     The same rule then disqualifies `AtomicU32` & co. for the wasm ATOMIC instructions, so their
#     atomicity comes from STRIPED LOCKS over those same byte-wise accesses instead. `Shared<T>`
#     keeps the method names and `Ordering` arguments `AtomicUN` had, so all 36 instruction bodies
#     and their endianness reasoning are unchanged.
#
#   memory.copy / memory.fill (builtin.rs, libcalls.rs, func_environ.rs)
#     Upstream carries FIXME(#4203) - "known to not be sound in the presence of shared memories" -
#     on both. They memmove/memset over memory another thread may be touching. Two new builtins do
#     the same work through byte-wise relaxed atomics, and the COMPILER chooses between them,
#     because only it knows whether the memory is shared: the libcall sees raw pointers. A private
#     memory keeps memmove and pays nothing. The small-constant inline expansion is also skipped
#     for a shared memory, since it expands to ordinary loads and stores.
#
#   pulley/src/interp.rs (tests)
#     Six tests for the mechanism itself rather than for bytecode, because the property is a MEMORY
#     MODEL property and the discriminating input is a racing thread.
#
# EVIDENCE, all on the box (NucBox_k11):
#
#   cargo check          pulley-interpreter --all-features, wasmtime-internal-cranelift, and
#                        wasmtime with threads: clean, no warnings in the files touched.
#   cargo test           6/6 pass natively.
#   MUTANT (no lock)     removing the guard from `Shared::rmw` makes the contention test fail
#                        39201 of 160000 - the lock is what the test depends on.
#   MIRI                 6/6 pass, no data race and no mixed-size access reported.
#   MIRI (old design)    restoring the width-sized fast path for aligned 4-byte accesses gives
#                        "Undefined Behavior: Race condition detected between (1) 4-byte atomic
#                        load on thread `unnamed-21` and (2) 1-byte atomic store on thread
#                        `unnamed-22`" - which is the whole reason for this change, reported by a
#                        tool rather than argued for in a comment.
